#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176842} :activity/id #uuid "92fe3122-f32d-5a2a-982c-2b7297621c95" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049134 :topic/id #uuid "dd8edb7c-3edb-53ed-9356-7a94bd4ed896" :topic/math-academy-id 3638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177005 :tutorial/content "When we form a random variable $Y$ from a linear combination of mutually independent, normally distributed random variables, the distribution of $Y$ is also normal.\n\nMore precisely, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *If $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are mutually independent normal random variables such that*\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim (\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2), \\qquad \\ldots, \\quad X_n\\sim(\\mu_n,\\sigma_n^2)\n$$\n\n> *and $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_n$ are real constants, then the linear combination*\n\n$$\nY=a_1X_1+a_2X_2+\\ldots+a_nX_n\n$$\n\n> *is normally distributed, where*\n> $Y\\sim N(a_1\\mu_1+a_2\\mu_2+\\ldots+a_n\\mu_n, \\: a_1^2\\sigma_1^2+a_2^2\\sigma_2^2+\\ldots+a_n^2\\sigma_n^2)$.\n\nFor example, if the random variables\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}3}), \\qquad X_2\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}4}), \\qquad X_3\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}1})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, then\n\n$$\nY= \\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{3}X_1+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{2}X_2+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{4}X_3\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\nLet's figure out the distribution of $Y$. We start by computing its mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= a_{1}μ_{1} + a_{2}μ_{2} + a_{3}μ_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 6 + 2 + 4 \\\\\n&= 12, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= a_{1}^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + a_{2}^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + a_{3}^{2}σ_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3^{2} \\cdot 3 + 2^{2} \\cdot 4 + 4^{2} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 4 + 16 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 27 + 16 + 16 \\\\\n&= 59\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = 3X_{1} + 2X_{2} + 4X_{3} \\mid ∼N(12, 59)\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It is vital that $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ are *mutually independent*. If they are *not*, their linear combination may *not* be normally distributed!" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7c68bf4-2275-5f3f-a765-fa30377c95a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5505 :tutorial/title "Linear Combinations of Normally Distributed Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "ad9619d2-b27f-51af-8cff-cfe8c0a50e17" :step/math-academy-id 34249 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176012 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175865 :question/id #uuid "6e7d897d-a59c-5944-b515-81132fd17715" :question/math-academy-id "e-9265"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3414fb92-7c59-5e59-af67-5c57b75d759f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049812}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176367}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum of Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f7daf50f-b20f-5c67-8c50-cae883d3f213" :step/math-academy-id 34662 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176013 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175866 :question/id #uuid "722126ae-cdef-5c3f-a031-f4ccefdc6fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9266"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc2565c7-5dcd-5573-9231-26c26cdae18c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176288}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Probabilities Involving Linear Combinations of Normally Distributed Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "88c1b73c-099d-5661-a71a-cfe8df532c98" :step/math-academy-id 34665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176038 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175685 :question/id #uuid "441d6816-7c83-52aa-b225-f2b108d4c906" :question/math-academy-id "e-10064"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71acfb6e-910a-5b33-8a4c-45867c3d69c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176365}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Linear Combination of Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d9f50537-814f-5027-b3e1-f89e6fce2a3e" :step/math-academy-id 37971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176039 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175686 :question/id #uuid "4b936a95-2418-5350-bef1-e13867b85bb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10065"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a579ddb-4754-5083-9bb2-e14e7bdfc19e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176289}] :knowledge-point/title "Combinations of Normally Distributed Random Variables in Context"} :step/id #uuid "c909f273-81c1-5ee1-9597-fb8ca7844bac" :step/math-academy-id 37972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177055 :tutorial/content "When we form a random variable $Y$ from a sum of **mutually independent**, normally distributed random variables, the distribution of $Y$ is also normal.\n\nMore precisely, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *If $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are mutually independent normal random variables such that*\n> $X_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim (\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2), \\qquad \\ldots, \\quad X_n\\sim(\\mu_n,\\sigma_n^2)$,\n> *then the random variable*\n> $Y=X_1+X_2+\\ldots+X_n$\n> *is normally distributed, where*\n> $Y\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2+\\ldots+\\mu_n, \\: \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2+\\ldots+\\sigma_n^2)$.\n\nFor example, if the random variables\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}3}), \\qquad X_2\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}4}), \\qquad X_3\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}1})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, then\n\n$$\nY=X_1+X_2+X_3\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\nLet's figure out the distribution of $Y$. We start by computing its mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= μ_{1} + μ_{2} + μ_{3} \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 4, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= σ_{1}^{2} + σ_{2}^{2} + σ_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3 + 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = X_{1} + X_{2} + X_{3} \\mid ∼N(4, 8)\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It is vital that $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ are *mutually independent*." :tutorial/id #uuid "d3a59008-5151-5f9b-b0cb-1645b06dcef8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6288 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3dee677e-7717-5f55-906f-98bc32493269" :step/math-academy-id 38095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176782}}] :activity/title "Combining Multiple Normally Distributed Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176155}]}
